1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:11,343
Last time four jobs and four candidates had exactly two stable matchings,

2
00:00:11,343 --> 00:00:15,448
one optimal for the jobs and one optimal for the candidates.

3
00:00:15,448 --> 00:00:20,974
So let us run propose and reject on these lists and see which one comes out.

4
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:26,842
On the first morning all four jobs ask Ada, because she is at the top of every list.

5
00:00:26,842 --> 00:00:34,216
Ada keeps job one, her favourite, and refuses the other three, and each of them crosses her off.

6
00:00:34,383 --> 00:00:42,887
On the second morning job one asks Ada again, job two asks Dora, job three asks Cleo, and job four asks Bea.

7
00:00:42,887 --> 00:00:50,007
Every candidate has exactly one offer, so nobody is refused and the algorithm stops.

8
00:00:50,183 --> 00:00:56,163
This is the first of our two matchings, the one in which every job has its optimal candidate.

9
00:00:56,163 --> 00:00:58,965
And look who did the refusing along the way.

10
00:00:58,965 --> 00:01:06,891
Jobs two, three and four were refused only by Ada, and Ada is not the optimal candidate of any of them.

11
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:09,884
So here is a bold guess for all lists.

12
00:01:09,884 --> 00:01:15,174
In propose and reject no job is ever refused by its optimal candidate.

13
00:01:15,174 --> 00:01:22,150
If that is true, every job ends with its optimal candidate, and the result is job optimal.

14
00:01:22,316 --> 00:01:24,028
Why would that be enough?

15
00:01:24,028 --> 00:01:30,734
A job only moves past a name that has refused it, so it never ends below its optimal candidate.

16
00:01:30,734 --> 00:01:34,885
And it cannot end above her, because the result is stable,

17
00:01:34,885 --> 00:01:38,534
and she is the best partner in any stable matching.

18
00:01:40,516 --> 00:01:43,398
Suppose the guess is wrong for some lists.

19
00:01:43,398 --> 00:01:49,660
Then there are days on which some job is refused by its optimal candidate, so mark those days red.

20
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:55,586
By the well ordering principle there is a first red day, and we look at that day.

21
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:59,844
On that day some job is refused by its optimal candidate.

22
00:01:59,844 --> 00:02:06,538
She refuses it because she keeps an offer she likes more, from another job, which we will call the rival.

23
00:02:06,538 --> 00:02:10,770
So she ranks the rival above the job she refused.

24
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:13,520
Now remember what optimal means.

25
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:17,746
She is the best partner the refused job has in any stable matching,

26
00:02:17,746 --> 00:02:21,658
so there is a stable matching in which these two are together.

27
00:02:21,658 --> 00:02:25,376
In that matching the rival has some partner too.

28
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:33,002
We will show that in this stable matching the candidate and the rival would both rather have each other.

29
00:02:33,002 --> 00:02:35,044
Her side of this is quick.

30
00:02:35,044 --> 00:02:40,942
There she is with the refused job, and we just saw that she ranks the rival above it.

31
00:02:41,116 --> 00:02:43,118
Now the side of the rival.

32
00:02:43,118 --> 00:02:49,222
On the first red day the rival is asking her, and a job asks the first name it has not crossed off.

33
00:02:49,222 --> 00:02:55,268
So every name above her on its list has already refused it, on an earlier day.

34
00:02:55,450 --> 00:03:00,336
But before the first red day no job was refused by its optimal candidate.

35
00:03:00,336 --> 00:03:04,884
So the optimal candidate of the rival is not among the names above her.

36
00:03:04,884 --> 00:03:09,752
That puts her level with the rival's optimal candidate or higher.

37
00:03:09,916 --> 00:03:13,494
Now look at the partner the rival has in the stable matching.

38
00:03:13,494 --> 00:03:16,759
That partner cannot be above its optimal candidate,

39
00:03:16,759 --> 00:03:20,728
because optimal means the best partner in any stable matching.

40
00:03:20,728 --> 00:03:25,064
So our candidate is level with that partner or higher.

41
00:03:25,216 --> 00:03:30,480
And she is not that partner, because in this matching she is with the refused job.

42
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:35,714
So the rival ranks her strictly above its partner, and wants to switch.

43
00:03:35,883 --> 00:03:38,974
So she prefers the rival and the rival prefers her,

44
00:03:38,974 --> 00:03:42,793
and they are a rogue couple inside a matching we called stable.

45
00:03:42,793 --> 00:03:44,975
That is a plain contradiction.

46
00:03:44,975 --> 00:03:52,965
So there is no first red day, no red day at all, and no job is ever refused by its optimal candidate.

47
00:03:53,116 --> 00:03:56,220
So every job ends with its optimal candidate,

48
00:03:56,220 --> 00:04:00,634
and propose and reject always produces the job optimal matching.

49
00:04:00,634 --> 00:04:07,388
The proof took the first red day and showed it cannot happen, which is induction in its well ordering form.

50
00:04:07,388 --> 00:04:12,242
And if the jobs get their best, what is left for the candidates?

